{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["<b>And Jacob was left alone.</b> Our Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded: he was left alone — this teaches that he went back for small jars, to teach that the money of the righteous is precious to them, so that they keep far from theft; thus explained Rashi, of blessed memory. And the intention is so that the small children would not be endangered on the road if they did not drink, and therefore he put himself in danger to go back, and the adversary immediately encountered him." "<b>And a man wrestled with him.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): like 'and he embraced' (ויחבק), the letters אחע״ה being interchangeable, and similarly the language of our Rabbis, of blessed memory: אבוקה is like חבוקה, on account of the logs that bind and embrace one another together." "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: ויאבק איש עמו — from the language of dust (אבק), for the man who was with him became filled with dust, and he was the guardian angel of Esav." "<b>And a matter</b> known: that were it not for sin, there would be no difference between man and angel, rather man would be greater than he, as our Rabbis said: the righteous are greater than the ministering angels. And therefore Scripture says that the angel sought to find in him a sin by which he might prevail over him, and did not find; and from this it says ״וירא כי לא יכול לו״ — he saw that he could not prevail against him — that is, he had no ability to overcome him on account of any sin, except on account of the sin of two sisters whom he married while they were both alive; this is ״ויגע בכף ירכו״ — and he struck the socket of his thigh — and the punishment was in the place of the sin. And ״ויגע בכף ירכו״ further includes that he struck the righteous who were destined to come forth from him — this is the generation of the persecution (shmad); and this is ״כי לא יכול״ — for he could not — to harm him, for he was not permitted in what he did except to dislocate the socket of his thigh." "And the matter was also an allusion to the generations: there would come a generation over whom Esav would prevail until he would be close to uprooting their very foundation, and this was in the generation of Rabbi Yehudah ben Bava and his colleagues. For thus our Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded: Rabbi Ḥiyya bar Abba said: If a person were to say to me, 'Give your life for the sanctification of the Name of the Holy One, blessed be He,' I would give it, provided they kill me at once; but in the generation of the persecution (shmad) I cannot endure. And what would they do in the generation of the persecution (shmad)? They would bring iron balls and heat them in the fire and place them under their armpits and take their lives from them."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:17:20.583508970Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}